   148	
   149	## A lease is facts, accreted
   150	
   151	No lease object exists. The record of a lease is its trace on a medium: at most
   152	one fact per negotiation status, any number of renewals, and — in the common
   153	case this vocabulary exists for — no terminal fact from the holder at all. Each
   154	fact is plain data on an ordinary stream. Keys live under `:dao.lease/…`, and
   155	maps are open in the usual way.
   156	
   157	Facts are classified by **two** dispatch keys, and a reader must switch on
   158	both: `:dao.lease/status` for the negotiation, `:dao.lease/event` for evidence.
   159	This mirrors the precedent's separation of its patch and trace streams by
   160	namespace.
   161	
   162	Following the precedent, the negotiation carries an explicit status. Each is
   163	authored exactly once, by the only party with authority to say it:
   164	
   165	| Fact      | `:dao.lease/status`   | Author  | Required beside `:dao.lease/lease`                                   |
   166	|-----------|-----------------------|---------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------|
   167	| Proposal  | `:dao.lease/proposed` | holder  | `:dao.lease/subject`; `:dao.lease/duration` optional — an ask          |
   168	| Grant     | `:dao.lease/accepted` | grantor | `:dao.lease/subject`, `:dao.lease/holder`, `:dao.lease/duration`; `:dao.lease/max` optional |
   169	| Refusal   | `:dao.lease/rejected` | grantor | —                                                                     |
   170	| Release   | `:dao.lease/released` | holder  | —                                                                     |
   171	| Reclaim   | `:dao.lease/lapsed`   | grantor | — (`:dao.lease/cause` optional: silence, cap, policy)                 |
   172	
   173	`:dao.lease/subject` names **what is leased** — the resource, in whatever plain
   174	data the domain uses to denote it. `:dao.lease/holder` names **to whom** —
   175	the author whose renewals count, in whatever form the judged medium's
   176	attribution takes. The grant carries both outright rather than referring back
   177	to a proposal, because a proposal may not exist, and because a reader of the
   178	grant alone must be able to say what was granted and to whom.
   179	
   180	Without the holder the attribution rules below are unenforceable in the only
   181	way that matters: a judge that cannot map lease to holder cannot reject a
   182	renewal from the wrong author, so any co-writer that learns a lease id can keep
   183	a dead holder's tenure alive. An unsolicited grant has no proposal whose
   184	authorship could be read back, so nothing else supplies it.
   185	
   186	`:dao.lease/lease` is the lease's identity, and **the grantor mints it**: it
   187	appears first on the grant, and every later fact naming the lease uses it. A
   188	holder-originated proposal cannot mint it, because a lease does not exist until
   189	it is granted; a proposal carries its own correlation id instead, which the
   190	grant echoes so a holder can tell which of its asks was answered. Minting by
   191	the grantor also settles uniqueness — the ids a judge reads are all its own —
   192	where identity minted by either party would leave two holders free to choose
   193	the same one.
   194	
   195	`:dao.lease/duration` is a plain-data quantity carrying its own unit — a map
   196	of unit to magnitude, not a bare number whose unit lives in a convention. It is
   197	positive: a grant whose duration is absent, zero, negative, or malformed is not
   198	a grant and establishes nothing, and a judge that reads one has read a
   199	defective fact rather than a lease. The comparison is strict, so an interval
   200	exactly equal to the duration has not yet lapsed.
   201	
   202	`:dao.lease/max` optionally caps total consecutive tenure, in the same
   203	representation, and a `max` below `duration` is likewise defective. Tolerance
   204	applies to the duration and **not** to the cap: tolerance exists to absorb
   205	flight time on evidence, and the cap is measured from the grantor's own
   206	observation of its own grant, where no flight occurs.
   207	
   208	**Only grantor-authored facts establish terms.** A holder cannot renew itself
   209	into tenure by asserting one, and a replayed or delayed holder fact establishes
   210	nothing. A proposal may carry a desired duration; it is advisory, exactly as a
   211	patch's replacement block is advisory until the VM accepts it. A grantor may
   212	grant less than was asked and owes no explanation.
   213	
   214	**A proposal creates no state.** An unanswered proposal is not pending and is
   215	not queued — it is simply not a lease. Nothing exists until `:accepted`, and a
   216	reader that finds a proposal with no acceptance has found a lease that was
   217	never granted. The statuses are a record of speech acts, not a state machine: a
   218	proposal is something someone said, not something the system holds.
   219	
   220	Three consequences follow, and each closes a question the vocabulary would
   221	otherwise leave open. A grantor **owes no answer** to a proposal; silence is
   222	its own sufficient response. `:dao.lease/rejected` is therefore courtesy in
   223	exactly the way `:dao.lease/released` is — it buys the holder a definitive
   224	negative it was never owed, and a system in which no refusal is ever appended
   225	is fully correct. And a holder's wait on an unanswered proposal is bounded by
   226	**nothing in this vocabulary**: it is an ordinary unanswered request, and how
   227	long to wait before proposing again is the holder's own policy. A proposal that
   228	was answered — accepted or refused — has spent its identity; renegotiating is a
   229	new proposal with a new lease id, since each status is authored exactly once.
   230	
   231	A grantor may also grant **unsolicited** — a lease whose first fact is
   232	`:accepted` — because the canonical case, a server leasing a per-connection
   233	composition, asks nobody first. Where a grant answers a proposal instead, it
   234	reaches the holder the same way every other fact does: the holder reads the
   235	medium the composition wired for it. This vocabulary provides no delivery
   236	mechanism and needs none.
   237	
   238	`:dao.lease/released` is the holder saying it is done. It is evidence, not a
   239	request and not an act: the grantor still performs the reclaim, on its own
   240	schedule, exactly as it would on a lapse. What release buys is promptness — the
   241	grantor need not wait out a duration of silence it already knows the reason
   242	for. A release from anyone but the lease's holder is not a release; a release
   243	after `:lapsed` is a late fact about a tenure already ended and changes nothing;
   244	and where release and lapse race, whichever the grantor acts on first is the
   245	one that terminates the lease, since both lead to the same idempotent reclaim.
   246	
   247	`:dao.lease/lapsed` is the reclaim record; its ordering with the act of
   248	reclaiming is specified under *Judging lapse*.
   249	
   250	There is no `:rolled-back`. The precedent's word is **renamed rather than
   251	omitted**, and the reason is connotation: rollback suggests undoing an
   252	application and restoring the world as if it had not happened, while a lapse
   253	ends a tenure whose entire past remains valid and undoes nothing. `:released`
   254	has no patch analogue at all, because an unapplied patch costs nothing while a
   255	holder done early saves the grantor up to one duration of waiting.
   256	
   257	## Renewal is evidence, not negotiation
   258	
   259	A renewal is the trace half:
   260	
   261	```clojure
   262	{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal
   263	 :dao.lease/lease <id>}
   264	```
   265	
   266	It carries no time and proposes nothing. It says one thing — *I still want
   267	this* — and the fact that it was appended, together with when it was observed,
   268	is its entire content. A renewal that asked for a new duration would be
   269	negotiating; renegotiation is a new proposal, not a fat renewal.
   270	
   271	**A renewal is a fact, not a claim on the future.** It obliges the grantor to
   272	nothing: the grantor re-judges at every judgment point and may stop honouring
   273	renewals at any moment without breaching anything, because the trace creates no
   274	obligations.
   275	
   276	**A renewal is never acknowledged**, so a holder never learns that its evidence
   277	was observed. Two rules follow for the holder, and both are its own discipline
   278	rather than anything the grantor enforces:
   279	
   280	- **Renew at a fraction of the duration** — half is the usual choice — and
   281	  treat the duration as a safety bound rather than a cadence.
   282	- **Stop acting at the bound.** A holder whose own clock has passed the
   283	  duration since its last renewal — **or since it observed the grant, whichever
   284	  is later** — must cease acting on what it holds, whether or not it has heard
   285	  anything. The grant seeds the holder's bound exactly as it seeds the judge's;
   286	  without that, a holder doing short work that never renews would have no bound
   287	  at all. Nothing in this vocabulary excludes a holder that keeps working past
   288	  its bound, and a paused agent that resumes after lapse acts on work the
   289	  grantor has already reclaimed and regranted.
   290	
   291	  This is damage limitation, not exclusion. The two windows do not coincide:
   292	  the judge's opens when *it* grants, the holder's when the grant *reaches* it,
   293	  and the difference is one delivery. A holder can therefore still be inside its
   294	  own bound after the grantor has reclaimed. Where a composition needs genuine
   295	  exclusion it must obtain it from the resource — a fencing token, a
   296	  single-writer medium, whatever the resource itself offers — because a lease
   297	  bounds attention and does not arbitrate access.
   298	
   299	## Attribution is a composition duty
   300	
   301	A renewal is evidence **about its author**, and a grant establishes terms only
   302	because the grantor authored it. Both depend on the judge being able to tell
   303	who wrote a fact — and **DaoStream does not supply that.** Its Concurrency
   304	section contemplates multiple writers and leaves one-log-per-writer to
   305	interpretation; a descriptor carries no authorization; and the attribution a
   306	WebSocket boundary provides is composed envelope data, not a property of the
   307	medium.
   308	
   309	So the composition owes it. A judged medium must give the judge a basis for
   310	authorship — one medium per author, an author key the boundary adds, or
   311	transport attribution as the WebSocket boundary provides.
   312	
   313	Unlike the obligation to judge, this one **is refusable at assembly**, and
   314	should be refused there. A judge is handed its medium, and a medium's
   315	attribution basis and eviction order are declared nature — inspectable exactly
   316	as a deposit destination's retention is, and refused exactly as the WebSocket
   317	boundary refuses a reject-mode destination. What cannot be inspected is whether
   318	anyone will ever judge; what can be is whether the medium they were given could
   319	support a judgment at all. Absent attribution, "only grantor-authored facts establish
   320	terms" is unenforceable: any writer can append an `:accepted` fact naming
   321	itself and take tenure. This is not a theoretical corner. Of the three uses
   322	that justify this vocabulary, only the served connection has attribution by
   323	construction; the forwarder pause and the shared-work claim run over in-process
   324	media where nothing distinguishes writers unless a composition arranges it.
   325	
   326	## Lapse is a conclusion; reclaim is an act
   327	
   328	Nothing fires when a lease lapses. The grantor runs an interpreter that reads
   329	the medium through its own cursor, stamps each fact it observes with the most
   330	recent tick it has seen, and judges: when the interval since its last relevant
   331	observation exceeds the granted duration plus whatever tolerance this judge
   332	allows, the guard has failed. This is the mark phase of a collector that cannot
   333	trace, and reclaim is its sweep — which is why the order below is fixed rather
   334	than incidental.
   335	
   336	Anyone may reach that conclusion; only the possessor's conclusion has effect.
   337	The interpreter composed with the resource — the one that can actually close
   338	the socket, stop the forwarder, free the entry — is the one whose judgment
   339	becomes a `:lapsed` fact. Two judges with different tolerances may disagree and
   340	neither is wrong; what settles it is **possession, not truth**, and that is
   341	what reclaiming *without consensus* means.
   342	
   343	**The judge is an interpreter over two streams.** One carries the lease facts.
   344	The other carries **ticks** — plain data appended by a host adapter when a
   345	timer fires, which is the ordinary form the governing document prescribes for
   346	turning a host event into stream data.
   347	
   348	What the tick stream supplies is **time as data**, not a wake-up. DaoStream
   349	invokes nothing and has no readiness extension, so a deposited tick rouses
   350	nobody; a driver still has to call `next`, and that driver is where cadence
   351	comes from. The distinction matters twice over. It keeps the clock out of lease
   352	code — a judge that called a host clock on each observation would be making the
   353	host call this document forbids, so **observation time is the most recent tick
   354	observed before the fact**, and the clock is read only by the adapter that
   355	deposits ticks. And it adds a term to the bound: the driver's own polling lag
   356	over the tick stream, which is why cadence must be declared as the interval
   357	between *completed judgment passes* rather than between timer deposits.
   358	
   359	Two cursors carry no ordering between them, so the pass is specified rather
   360	than left to the scheduler. At each judgment pass the judge, in this order:
   361	
   362	1. **Drains the lease-fact cursor to `:dao.stream/blocked`**, updating every
   363	   observation it finds. A tick judged while an already-deposited renewal sits
   364	   unread is a false lapse manufactured by scheduling.
   365	2. **Judges.** Compares each lease's interval against its duration and
   366	   tolerance.
   367	3. **Reclaims.** Performs the act — close, stop, free — which must be
   368	   idempotent, because it may be retried.
   369	4. **Appends `:lapsed`.** The record follows the act, mark before sweep before
   370	   record.
   371	
   372	**The record goes to the grantor's own log**, not to the holder. In the
   373	canonical case the reclaim act *is* closing the connection, so an append aimed
   374	at the holder would fail every time rather than rarely: the grantor's record
   375	and the holder's signal are different facts on different streams, and the
   376	holder learns what happened from its own medium (for a served connection, an
   377	ordinary `:ws/closed`).
   378	
   379	Even on the grantor's own log the record is evidence that a reclaim was
   380	performed, never a promise that one will be. If the append fails, the resource
   381	is reclaimed and unrecorded; the lease stays in the judge's pending-reclaim set
   382	and the append is retried, and readers must treat the absence of a `:lapsed`
   383	fact as no evidence of continued tenure.
   384	
   385	## Whose clock decides
   386	
   387	**Durations cross boundaries; moments never do.** A grant carries a duration, a
   388	renewal carries nothing, and no expiry timestamp appears in any fact.
   389	
   390	The judge measures intervals between its own observations, so **offset skew
   391	cannot enter the comparison** — it happens entirely on one clock. **Rate skew
   392	remains**: a judge whose clock runs fast relative to the holder's shortens the
   393	real tolerance below what the holder expects, and that is a false lapse with
   394	zero transit delay. The renewal fraction absorbs it, which makes the sizing
   395	relation a skew budget as well as a load budget.
   396	
   397	Transit delay cuts both ways, and conflating them is how tolerance gets sized
   398	to zero. **While a renewal is in flight it protects nothing**: the interval
   399	since the previous observation keeps growing, and a judgment pass inside the
   400	flight window false-lapses a holder that renewed on time. That is what the
   401	tolerance must cover, and why the renewal fraction exists as margin. **Once the
   402	renewal is observed** the window is re-seeded from that later observation, so
   403	the same delay then extends real tenure — safe for the holder, and unsafe for
   404	the scarce resource, since a late-observed renewal from an already-dead holder
   405	buys a fresh duration. The evidence carries no generation or sequence, and its
   406	arrival is its entire meaning. This document takes that trade deliberately,
   407	preferring to extend a dead tenure over reclaiming a live one.
   408	
   409	**Continuity is lost when no eligible renewal is observed before a completed
   410	judgment pass finds the interval past duration plus tolerance.** Partition
   411	length alone does not decide it: a partition longer than the duration can be
   412	survived when tolerance and tick phase cover the excess, and a shorter one can
   413	still be fatal depending on when it begins relative to the last observation. No
   414	discipline available here prevents the fatal case — a `:dao.stream/ok` on an
   415	outbound path implies nothing about delivery — and the holder's remedy is
   416	renegotiation when it reconnects.
   417	
   418	Sizing is stated as relations, never numbers, because the numbers belong to
   419	compositions: the medium's retention window exceeds the judge's lag; the
   420	tolerance covers expected flight time and rate skew; the granted duration
   421	exceeds twice the holder's renewal interval.
   422	
   423	## Judging lapse
   424	
   425	The judge keeps, as private state, for every lease it has granted:
   426	
   427	- the **terms** — subject, holder, duration, cap;
   428	- the **tenure start**, the observation of the grant, which `:dao.lease/max` is
   429	  measured from and which no renewal overwrites;
   430	- the observation of the **most recent relevant fact**, the grant or the newest
     1	# Datom-Native VM Execution Trace Surface for JIT Observation and Runtime Optimization
     2	
     3	## Summary
     4	Introduce a first-class execution trace stream for VM runs, represented as datoms (`[e a v t m]`), so JIT can observe runtime behavior without hidden callbacks or mutable side channels.  
     5	JIT remains advisory: it emits optimization patch datoms, and VM applies them only through explicit, causal patch-stream processing.
     6	
     7	## Surface API (Decision Complete)
     8	Add a trace protocol and kernel helpers in `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc`.
     9	
    10	1. New protocol: `IVMTrace`
    11	- `run-traced [vm opts]`
    12	- Contract: executes VM to halt/blocked and returns:
    13	  `{:vm final-vm :trace trace-datoms :run-id run-eid}`
    14	
    15	2. New protocol method for stepwise tracing:
    16	- `step-traced [vm opts]`
    17	- Contract: executes one VM step and returns:
    18	  `{:vm next-vm :trace-step step-datoms :run-id run-eid :step step-idx}`
    19	
    20	3. Keep existing `vm/run` unchanged
    21	- `vm/run` remains non-observability baseline for compatibility and low overhead.
    22	- JIT and tooling use `run-traced` or repeated `step-traced`.
    23	
    24	4. Trace stream access pattern
    25	- Bounded run stream: `(:trace (vm/run-traced ...))`
    26	- Open stream behavior: repeated `step-traced` calls produce append-only step streams in `t` order.
    27	
    28	## Trace Datom Schema (What State Goes in Stream)
    29	Define trace attributes (new namespace, for example `:yin.trace/*`) and emit only JIT-relevant state.
    30	
    31	1. Run-level facts
    32	- `[:run-eid :yin.trace/type :yin.trace/run t m]`
    33	- `[:run-eid :yin.trace/vm-kind :register|:stack|:semantic t m]`
    34	- `[:run-eid :yin.trace/program-id <hash-or-id> t m]`
    35	- `[:run-eid :yin.trace/start-ip <int> t m]`
    36	- `[:run-eid :yin.trace/end-reason :halted|:blocked|:error t m]`
    37	
    38	2. Step-level facts (one step entity per executed instruction)
    39	- `[:step-eid :yin.trace/run :run-eid t m]`
    40	- `[:step-eid :yin.trace/idx <int> t m]`
    41	- `[:step-eid :yin.trace/opcode <keyword|int> t m]`
    42	- `[:step-eid :yin.trace/ip-before <int> t m]`
    43	- `[:step-eid :yin.trace/ip-after <int> t m]`
    44	- `[:step-eid :yin.trace/reg-read [r...] t m]`
    45	- `[:step-eid :yin.trace/reg-write [r...] t m]`
    46	- `[:step-eid :yin.trace/branch-taken true|false t m]` (when branch op)
    47	- `[:step-eid :yin.trace/call-target <symbol|closure-id|primitive-id> t m]` (when call)
    48	- `[:step-eid :yin.trace/argc <int> t m]` (when call)
    49	- `[:step-eid :yin.trace/ret-val-tag <type-tag> t m]` (when return)
    50	- `[:step-eid :yin.trace/deopt-guard-failed? true|false t m]` (if guard exists)
    51	
    52	3. Periodic frame snapshots for reconstruction (not every step)
    53	- `[:snap-eid :yin.trace/run :run-eid t m]`
    54	- `[:snap-eid :yin.trace/step-idx <int> t m]`
    55	- `[:snap-eid :yin.trace/ip <int> t m]`
    56	- `[:snap-eid :yin.trace/regs <compact-reg-view> t m]`
    57	- `[:snap-eid :yin.trace/env-shape <shape-id> t m]`
    58	- `[:snap-eid :yin.trace/k-depth <int> t m]`
    59	
    60	4. Minimal required tags for JIT specialization
    61	- Value type tags only by default (`:int`, `:float`, `:bool`, `:closure`, `:nil`, `:map`, `:vec`, etc.)
    62	- No full value payload unless explicitly enabled in opts.
    63	
    64	## JIT Patch Stream (Advisory Authority)
    65	JIT emits a separate patch stream, never direct mutation.
    66	
    67	1. Patch datoms example attributes (`:yin.jit/*`)
    68	- `:yin.jit/target-program-id`
    69	- `:yin.jit/target-ip-range`
    70	- `:yin.jit/guard` (shape/type predicates)
    71	- `:yin.jit/replacement` (optimized block reference)
    72	- `:yin.jit/version`
    73	- `:yin.jit/status :proposed|:accepted|:rejected|:rolled-back`
    74	
    75	2. VM application model
    76	- VM checks patch stream at explicit safe points (function entry, loop header, backward jump target, return boundary).
    77	- On accepted patch, VM switches to new versioned block with guard.
    78	- On guard failure, VM emits deopt datom and falls back to baseline bytecode.
    79	
    80	## Options/Defaults (Locked)
    81	For `run-traced` and `step-traced`:
    82	- `:snapshot-every` default `64` steps
    83	- `:emit-values?` default `false`
    84	- `:emit-env?` default `:shape-only`
    85	- `:max-trace-steps` default `nil` (no cap)
    86	- `:trace-level` default `:jit-core` (fields listed above)
    87	
    88	## Implementation Plan (Files)
    89	1. `src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc`
    90	- Add `IVMTrace` protocol.
    91	- Add shared helpers for run-id, step-id, and trace datom emitters.
    92	
    93	2. `src/cljc/yin/vm/register.cljc`
    94	- Implement `step-traced` by instrumenting `step-bc` boundary.
    95	- Implement `run-traced` as loop over `step-traced`.
    96	- Keep `reg-vm-run` unchanged.
    97	
    98	3. `src/cljc/yin/vm/stack.cljc` and `src/cljc/yin/vm/semantic.cljc` (optional phase 2)
    99	- Implement same protocol for cross-backend consistency.
   100	- If phased rollout: register VM first, others return `ex-info` “trace not implemented yet”.
   101	
   102	4. `test/yin/vm/register_test.cljc`
   103	- Add tests asserting trace causality and required fields.
   104	- Keep existing `compile-and-run-bc` tests unchanged.
   105	
   106	## Test Cases and Scenarios
   107	1. Trace determinism
   108	- Same bytecode + env yields same opcode/ip sequence trace.
   109	
   110	2. Step/run consistency
   111	- Concatenated `step-traced` events equals `run-traced` events for same input.
   112	
   113	3. Causality correctness
   114	- `t` strictly monotonic within run.
   115	- `m` links step datoms to run entity and patch/deopt causes.
   116	
   117	4. JIT signal sufficiency
   118	- Hot loop program produces repeated `(ip-before, opcode)` signatures.
   119	- Branch-heavy program records `branch-taken` distribution.
   120	- Polymorphic call site records multiple `call-target`/type tags.
   121	
   122	5. Patch safety
   123	- Accepted patch emits status transitions and version facts.
   124	- Guard failure triggers deopt event and baseline fallback with correct final value.
   125	
   126	6. Backward compatibility
   127	- Existing `vm/run` behavior and current tests remain unchanged.
   128	
   129	## Assumptions and Chosen Defaults
   130	1. Canonical observability format is datoms, not callbacks or mutable listeners.
   131	2. JIT is advisory and cannot mutate active execution implicitly.
   132	3. Default trace is compact and type-tag oriented to limit overhead.
   133	4. Register VM is the first backend to implement tracing, others follow same interface.
   134	5. `vm/run` remains untouched for compatibility and low-overhead execution paths.
