    53	|--------------|------------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
    54	| `create!`    | —                                              | Absent: the transport owns no stream. Dynamic dispatch for this type returns `not-found`.     |
    55	| `attach!`    | `ok`, `invalid-descriptor`, `transport-error`  | `transport-error` covers only failures decidable here and now — no socket API on this host, a constructor that fails, local resources exhausted. `not-found`, and the reachability failures that need a remote answer, are displaced to the deposit medium. |
    56	| `descriptor` | `ok`                                           | — (the contract's whole set)                                                                  |
    57	| `append!`    | `ok`, `full`, `invalid-value`, `closed`, `transport-error` | — (the contract's whole set)                                                      |
    58	| `close!`     | `ok`                                           | — (the contract's whole set)                                                                  |
    59	| `cursor`, `next` | —                                          | No reader surface, per above.                                                                 |
    60	
    61	## The Duplex Model
    62	
    63	A WebSocket is two independent directions sharing one connection, and the
    64	design keeps them separate:
    65	
    66	- **Outbound**: `append!` on the socket handle encodes the value and
    67	  sends it. Sending is appending; nothing is converted.
    68	- **Inbound**: each arriving message is a host event. The host's adapter
    69	  deposits it — payloads and connection lifecycle events alike, judging
    70	  nothing — onto the stream the **host composition** wired for this
    71	  boundary. The deposit medium is an ordinary DaoStream stream the host
    72	  composed; this transport defines no separate event mechanism and no bus of
    73	  its own. (In the v2 migration slice that stream is a ring buffer, under a
    74	  time-boxed exception recorded in ADR 0003; the adapter cannot tell what it
    75	  deposits into, so the later swap is composition-only.) From that moment the traffic has positions, retention, cursors,
    76	  and gaps — the reading model of the contract applies in full, on that
    77	  stream.
    78	
    79	The deposit destination is wired once, when the host composes the boundary —
    80	it is not a parameter of `attach!`, and no attaching caller chooses it. The
    81	composition that wires the boundary also hands the medium to whichever
    82	interpreter reads it, which may or may not be the code that called `attach!`. Every interpreter takes its own perspective on the same deposited
    83	truth: one indexes the traffic, another copies it to a file, a third ignores
    84	it. How arrivals are interpreted is the interpreter's business, not the
    85	transport's configuration.
    86	
    87	**Attachment identity.** This transport distinguishes its attachments, so its
    88	`attach!` success map carries `:dao.stream/attachment` as the contract
    89	requires: a descriptor names the served stream and cannot tell two attachments
    90	to it apart, and a handle is a host-local object that cannot be compared
    91	against data that crossed a codec.
    92	
    93	Every event deposited for that attachment carries the same value under
    94	`:ws/attachment`. The key differs because the scopes differ: result keywords
    95	are the contract's and live under `:dao.stream/…`, while a deposited event is
    96	a stream element, where transport-owned keys live under the transport's
    97	namespace. Correlation is by the value carried, never by the key's name.
    98	
    99	Without that identity a shared medium is a multiplexed sequence with no
   100	demultiplexing key: every event present, in order, and unattributable.
   101	
   102	**Deposited events are envelopes**, so that payload and lifecycle are
   103	distinguishable and both are attributable:
   104	
   105	```clojure
   106	{:ws/attachment <id>
   107	 :ws/event      :ws/payload  ; or :ws/opened :ws/closed :ws/ended
   108	                             ;    :ws/error :ws/not-found
   109	                             ;    :ws/transport-error
   110	 :ws/value      <decoded>}   ; payload events only
   225	stream deliberately. The handshake's disclaimer is what makes every other case
   226	resolve as soon as a server is reachable. Neither is sufficient alone.
   227	
   228	### Deposit Admission
   229	
   230	The adapter runs in a callback context: it cannot wait, cannot park, and
   231	cannot record a refused deposit in the medium that refused it. So the
   232	deposit destination must be one that always admits, and this is enforced
   233	where it can be — at assembly, against the destination's declared nature:
   234	
   235	- The destination must declare **evict-oldest retention with a
   236	  host-declared capacity**. It never answers `:dao.stream/full`; under
   237	  pressure, loss surfaces as reported `gap`s at lagging readers — the one
   238	  loss mode the contract blesses. Unbounded in-memory retention is not an
   239	  alternative: it trades a refused deposit for eventual host death, the
   240	  most silent failure of all. (A genuinely durable medium — a disk-backed
   241	  log — plays the same game with a much larger declared window; exhausting
   242	  it at runtime surfaces as `:dao.stream/transport-error` on the deposit,
   243	  like any storage failure.)
   244	- The destination must also be able to **carry every envelope this transport
   245	  deposits** — attachment identifiers, lifecycle events, and every value the
   246	  codec admits. A destination that evicts oldest but answers
   247	  `:dao.stream/invalid-value` on some envelope is as unwired as a reject-mode
   248	  one; retention mode alone is not admission.
   249	- Wiring a reject-mode destination is a **host assembly defect**, refused
   250	  when the boundary is composed. Throwing is reserved for defects detected there, before
   251	  the boundary operates; everything at deposit time is data.
   252	- The destination must outlive the boundary, and **every non-`ok` deposit
   253	  result tears the connection down**: `closed` means the destination is
   254	  gone; `full` or `invalid-value` from a supposedly conforming destination
   255	  is a host or transport defect, evidenced by the returned data;
   256	  `transport-error` is runtime storage failure. In every case, a boundary
   257	  that can no longer turn events into data stops claiming to be attached.
   258	  Disconnection is fully observable — the peer's boundary deposits the
   259	  departure, the local handle answers `:dao.stream/closed`, and
   260	  re-attaching after the host repairs its composition is ordinary
   261	  `attach!`.
   262	
   263	Sizing the window is choosing how much reader lag to tolerate before gaps
   264	appear — an explicit engineering decision in the host's composition. Whether
   265	that window can be resized after the fact is the destination transport's
   266	declared nature, not this transport's business. An adaptive policy — grow on
   267	rising gap rate, shrink when quiet — is an interpreter reading its own
   268	telemetry, not a transport behavior.
   269	
   270	The accounting is then honest, though not complete: declared retention
   271	pressure surfaces as reader-visible `gap`s, and a dead destination tears the
   272	attachment down. But a deposit that fails is a deposit that did not land, and
   273	neither can the teardown event land in the destination that just refused it —
   274	so in that one case the local failure is observable only through the deposit
   275	result the adapter holds and through subsequent handle operations, and the
   276	peer observes it only if teardown reaches the wire.
   277	
   278	## Envelope
   279	
   280	Transport-owned keys, qualified under `:ws/…`. The exact key set is TBD
   300	and never the vocabulary of asking and disclaiming.
   301	
   302	Their exact form is **not specified yet**; see Deferred. Until it is, two
   303	independently written implementations cannot interoperate and no wire-level
   304	conformance test can be written.
   305	
   306	## Operations
   307	
   308	- There is no `create!`. The transport owns no stream, so it creates none;
   309	  a host serves a stream it composed elsewhere, and starting an endpoint is
   310	  a host composition step, not a DaoStream operation. A creation
   311	  specification naming this transport returns `:dao.stream/not-found`.
   312	- `attach!` begins connecting and returns without waiting, per the
   313	  contract's rule that no operation waits. It answers what is decidable
   314	  locally, at the moment it is called: `:dao.stream/invalid-descriptor` for
   315	  a descriptor it cannot read, `:dao.stream/transport-error` for a handler
   316	  that fails here and now — no socket API on this host, a constructor that
   317	  fails, local resources exhausted — and otherwise `:dao.stream/ok` with a
   318	  handle on an attachment still being established. It never returns an
   319	  answer it has not heard. This is not a concession to convenience: on a host with no
   320	  blocking IO the endpoint's answer cannot arrive before `attach!` returns,
   321	  and an `attach!` that waited for it would have to hand back a promise —
   322	  breaking the result convention on every host that cannot block, and giving
   323	  the same transport different outcome sets per host.
   324	- **How the connection resolved arrives as data**, on the same host-composed
   325	  medium as every other socket event, correlated by attachment identity.
   326	  The distinction survives intact, only its channel moves. These are
   327	  deposited elements, not outcome maps, so they carry the transport's own
   328	  vocabulary, and exactly one resolution event is deposited per attachment:
   329	  `:ws/opened` when the endpoint accepted the stream, and otherwise one of the
   330	  two failures. `:ws/not-found` is the authoritative answer that what the
   331	  descriptor names does not exist — the endpoint responded and disclaimed
   332	  the stream — and is not retried automatically (though a server may serve
   333	  that identity later; retrying on external evidence is interpreter
   334	  policy), while `:ws/transport-error` covers transient
   335	  reachability and connection failures — DNS, timeout, routing, refusal —
   336	  and retrying it may succeed on its own. A caller's retry policy is as
   337	  deterministic as before; it reads the answer from the medium instead of
   338	  from a return value.
   339	- `append!` returns `:dao.stream/full` when the send buffer cannot accept
   340	  the value now — **transient**: the network drains the buffer on its own,
   341	  and a later append succeeds. An attachment still establishing its
   342	  connection answers `full` for the same reason and with the same remedy.
   343	  `:dao.stream/closed` once the connection is down, which includes an
   344	  attachment whose connection never succeeded. A value that cannot be
   345	  encoded for the wire is `:dao.stream/invalid-value`. A local send that
   346	  fails for neither reason is `:dao.stream/transport-error`. Operating the
   347	  handle therefore tells a caller **that** the attachment is not writable,
   348	  needing no reader at all — but never why: an authoritative `not-found` and
   349	  a transient failure both reduce to `closed` here, and the distinction
   350	  survives only as the correlated **resolution** event on the deposit medium
   351	  — never as a lifecycle event, which is precisely the class that does not
   352	  carry it.
   353	- `close!` on an attached handle means **disconnect from the server**: the
   354	  handle is on the attachment, so closing it ends the connection,
   355	  idempotently, and this handle's subsequent `append!` returns
   356	  `:dao.stream/closed`. The served stream is untouched — closing *it*
   357	  belongs to its server-side owner. Disconnection is not final: the
   358	  descriptor remains valid and `attach!` rejoins the same stream. The
   359	  peer's boundary deposits the departure into its host-composed event
   360	  medium, correlated by attachment identity.
   361	- Connection loss is the same detachment, uninvited: the served stream
   362	  persists, its descriptor remains valid, and `attach!` with the same
   363	  descriptor rejoins the same stream. What a client must not confuse with it
   364	  is the served stream *ending*, which is a different fact reaching it by a
   365	  different route (see Ending a served stream).
   366	
   367	## Elements and Serialization
   368	
   369	The wire is a serialization boundary. Every element must survive the codec
   370	structurally unchanged; a handle can never travel — its portable
   371	descriptor can.
   372	
   373	## Deferred
   374	
   375	- Exact envelope key set (tracks the contract's descriptor TBD).
   376	- Handshake wire format: how the served stream's identity is presented on
   377	  connect, the exact form of the authoritative disclaimer, and the
   378	  application-range close code that distinguishes an ended stream from a
   379	  dropped connection (see Ending a served stream).
   380	- The value codec. The contract requires elements to survive it structurally
