## Findings

No critical findings.

1. **High | [A lease is facts, accreted, lines 151–193 and 214–229](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:151) | The proposal identity schema contradicts itself.** The table requires every status—including `:proposed` and `:rejected`—to carry `:dao.lease/lease`, while lines 186–190 say that key first exists on the grant and proposals instead carry an unnamed correlation ID. Line 229 then again calls the identity of a new proposal a “lease id.” Acceptance and refusal are also not declared mutually exclusive.  
   **Exact correction:** Define a named proposal key, for example `:dao.lease/proposal`. Require it on proposals and responses. Require `:dao.lease/lease` only on accepted grants and subsequent lease facts. State that exactly one of accepted/rejected may answer a proposal, define duplicate/conflicting-answer handling, and replace “new lease id” at line 229 with “new proposal id.”

2. **High | [Renewal is evidence, lines 271–297](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:271) and [Renewal forever, lines 594–608](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:594) | The document both says renewals maintain tenure and says the grantor may ignore them at any moment.** If a renewal creates no obligation and policy may reclaim immediately, the duration grants the holder no minimum tenure. That contradicts “actively maintained,” “renewing forever holds the lease forever,” and the holder-side safety discipline. What remains is a revocable heartbeat hint, not the lease described elsewhere.  
   **Exact correction:** Choose one contract. Either require the grantor to honor eligible renewals until the duration or cap expires, reserving early revocation for a separately named resource policy; or explicitly rename and reframe this as advisory liveness evidence and remove every claim that renewal maintains tenure.

3. **High | [Lapse is a conclusion, lines 328–370](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:328) | The judgment pass never reads the tick stream.** The judge is said to interpret two streams, but the normative four-step algorithm drains only lease facts and then judges. It therefore does not define how “now” advances. It also leaves tick representation, monotonicity, unit conversion, regression, and tick-stream gaps unspecified. An implementer must invent the clock semantics on which correctness and the reclamation bound depend.  
   **Exact correction:** Define ticks as monotonic local readings with a specified duration-compatible representation and regression policy. Make each pass explicitly capture/drain ticks to a single judgment instant, drain lease facts and stamp them consistently with that instant, then judge, reclaim, and record. Specify what a tick-stream gap means.

4. **High | [Judging lapse, lines 458–503](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:458) | “Reclaim and re-grant” does not actually work without history.** After losing unpersisted terms, the grantor no longer knows the leased subject, old lease, holder, or destination for the new unsolicited grant. “For every resource it still possesses” silently introduces an authoritative resource/allocation inventory, and “needs no history at all” is therefore false. This relocates the restart defect outside the named root set.  
   **Exact correction:** Make reclaim-and-regrant conditional on an independently durable authoritative inventory containing the resource, reclaim operation, and new-holder/delivery decision. Otherwise state that a judge which lost its terms cannot identify or re-grant old leases. Remove “needs no history at all.”

5. **High | [Attribution is a composition duty, lines 299–324](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:299) | Generic assembly refusal is overstated.** DaoStream declares surfaces, outcomes, and retention behavior, but supplies neither author identity nor a standard operation for inspecting an “attribution basis.” One-medium-per-author is useful only if the composition separately binds that medium to an author. The current wording makes a security precondition sound automatically inspectable when it is not.  
   **Exact correction:** Require the judge constructor to receive an explicit attribution resolver or a declared source-to-author binding. Assembly may refuse only when that supplied evidence is absent or incompatible; do not attribute this capability to DaoStream itself.

6. **Medium | [Collector evidence, lines 55–82](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:55) and [Judging lapse, lines 443–447](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:443) | The GC terminology overclaims and misclassifies the judge’s state.** A tracing collector computes reachability, not whether an object is “wanted”; reachable garbage exists. Its roots are starting references, not the set of every allocated object. Calling every granted lease a root set would, under actual GC semantics, keep every lease reachable forever. The state is an active-grant ledger. Its legitimacy comes from being owner-scoped and stream-persisted, not from GC lineage.  
   **Exact correction:** Replace “wanted” with “reachable from the declared roots” and qualify “never wrong” as “does not free reachable objects when roots and tracing are correct.” Rename the judge’s “root set” to “active-grant ledger” or “working set” and justify it directly from ownership and persistence boundaries.

7. **Medium | [Collector/JIT framing, lines 31–82 and 326–334](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:31), [Delegated renewal, lines 610–662](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:610) | GC does not presently justify three of the four things claimed for it.** It usefully explains why silence is incomplete evidence and why reclamation should be conservative. “Mark before sweep” merely renames the already-required conclude-before-act causality; no reachability marking occurs. The delegation denial is independently reasoned and contains no reference-cycle parallel. The fence also says nothing explicitly preventing expectations of transitive reachability or cycle collection.  
   **Exact correction:** Keep GC as lineage for the timeout/reachability substitution, but call lapse evaluation “classification,” not a mark phase. Add a fence stating that leases neither traverse references nor discover/collect cycles. Do not claim GC supports delegation denial; retain the stronger authorship and false-liveness argument already present.

8. **Medium | [Duration schema, lines 195–206](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:195) and [sizing, lines 418–421](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/dao.lease.md:418) | Time quantities remain insufficiently specified and two restrictions conflict.** “A map of unit to magnitude” does not define allowed units, cardinality, numeric domain, normalization, overflow, or comparison across units. Tolerance has no schema at all. Also, “half is the usual choice” yields equality while the sizing rule demands duration be strictly greater than twice the interval. Rejecting `max < duration` is unnecessary: the cap can validly terminate tenure first.  
   **Exact correction:** Define one canonical duration/tolerance schema and comparison procedure. Require tolerance to be non-negative and unit-compatible. Say renewal interval is at most half the duration—or strictly less if that margin is intended. Permit any positive cap and lapse at the earlier applicable bound.

No low-severity findings.

## Was the GC reframe worth making?

**Yes.** The sentence “a duration is a timeout standing in for reachability” cleanly explains the design’s central epistemic limitation and its conservative treatment of gaps and restarts.

In its current form, however, the reframe earns its space only for that point. The root-set, mark-phase, and delegation/cycle claims should be narrowed or removed.

## The thing most likely wrong that I cannot prove is wrong

The “claim an agent leaves on shared work” is likely a durable resource, which would place one of the document’s three motivating uses behind the durable-judge, single-incarnation, and resource-fencing requirements that this design explicitly does not supply. I cannot prove that without the intended shared-work composition, but it may mean the document presently supports only two of its three claimed uses.
